    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0113",
      "sequence": 968,
      "meaning": "A celestial hymn hails Rāma as the abode of beauty and rest for those who bow, mighty-armed bearer of bow, arrows and quiver; enemy of Dūṣaṇa and Khara, destroyer of demon hosts, remover of earth’s burden and foe of Rāvaṇa. The speaker recalls how proud Laṅkā’s lord subjugated gods, gandharvas, sages, siddhas, humans, birds and nāgas and obstructed every path, and says the malicious sinner has obtained his fruit. Confessing former pride and now humbled by the painful sight of Rāma’s lotus feet, the worshipper leaves others to meditate upon formless Brahman but chooses Rāma’s embodied form as king of Kosala. He asks the Lord to dwell in his heart with Vaidehī and Lakṣmaṇa and grant devotion, then bows to the tender, compassionate Rāma served by Brahmā and Śiva.",
