    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0114",
      "sequence": 969,
      "meaning": "Rāma asks Indra to revive the monkeys and bears slain by demons for his sake. Śiva tells Garuḍa that the Lord’s words are unfathomable even to learned sages: Rāma himself can destroy and revive the three worlds, but gives the honour to Indra. A rain of nectar restores the bears and monkeys, who rise joyfully and come to the Lord. Though ambrosia falls on both armies, no demons revive: with minds assuming Rāma’s form at death, they were released from worldly bonds. The bears and monkeys, portions of gods embodied for service, live again by Raghupati’s will. No one equals Rāma as friend of the helpless—he liberated the whole mass of demons, even corrupt and lustful Rāvaṇa, granting a destiny rare for great sages.",
